    "q-94733": {
      "dom_id": "step-q94733",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "$$\n\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $x$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be negative-definite.\n\nI. $R(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}$\n\nII. $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{aligned}]x$\n\nIII. $T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$",
      "worked_solution": "A quadratic form $Q(x)$ is negative-definite if $Q(x)<0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖\\{0\\}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms by substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n- Quadratic form I *cannot* be negative-definite since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={5}^{2}+2(0)^{2} \\\\  & =25+0 \\\\  & =25>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form II *might* be negative-definite. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-50<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form III *cannot* be negative-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x) & ={5}^{2}-{0}^{2}+2\\cdot 5\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =25+0 \\\\  & =25>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
