    "q-94738": {
      "dom_id": "step-q94738",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Which of the following statements regarding quadratic forms are true?\n\nI. $R(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}$ is positive-definite\n\nII. $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x$ is indefinite\n\nIII. $T(x)=-{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}$ is negative-definite",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that, a quadratic form $Q(x)$ is:\n\n- positive-definite if $Q(x)>0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖\\{0\\};$\n\n- negative-definite if $Q(x)<0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖\\{0\\};$\n\n- indefinite if \n\n- $Q({x}_{1})>0$ for some ${x}_{1}\\in {R}^{n},$ and\n\n- $Q({x}_{2})<0$ for some ${x}_{2}\\in {R}^{n}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖\\{0\\},$ the quadratic form is positive-definite.\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting ${x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S({x}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting ${x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S({x}_{2}) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-4<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic form is indefinite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x)=-{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖\\{0\\},$ the quadratic form is negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
