    "q-139434": {
      "dom_id": "step-q139434",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "$$\n\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$ By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $x$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be positive-definite.\n\nI. $R(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$\n\nII. $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]x$\n\nIII. $T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$",
      "worked_solution": "A quadratic form $Q(x)$ is positive-definite if $Q(x)>0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖\\{0\\}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms by substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n- Quadratic form I *might* be positive-definite. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}-2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =1+9+6 \\\\  & =16>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form II *might* be positive-definite. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =16>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form III *cannot* be positive-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x) & ={1}^{2}-(-3)^{2}+1\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =1-9-3 \\\\  & =-11<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
