    "q-94806": {
      "dom_id": "step-q94806",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=-6$ and $det(A)<0$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is negative-definite",
      "worked_solution": "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-6$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-6<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-6<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}|=2>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-6<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
