    "q-94467": {
      "dom_id": "step-q94467",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=-1$ and $det(A)>0$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is positive-definite",
      "worked_solution": "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-1$ but \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-1<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}|=2>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-4<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
